  "ruling": {
    "ruling": "NO_ACTION",
    "amount_sgd": null,
    "driver_compensation_sgd": null,
    "referrals": [],
    "confidence": 0.85,
    "explanation": "To the rider and to the driver: I have gone through the trace, the trip record, the chat log and the fare ledger, and the platform's fare stands unchanged. No money moves.\n\nThe road — no deviation. The GPS trace is complete: 133 fixes at a 10-second cadence, 100% coverage, no gaps, admissibility weight 1.00. It measures 15.79 km against a booked optimal of 15.5 km, an excess of 0.29 km, or 1.87%. POL-RD-01 requires the excess to exceed both 0.8 km and 20% of the optimal distance; neither is met, so no route deviation is established and no distance refund arises. The rider concedes this, and the driver's reading of the same figures is accepted. A traffic advisory is on file only as a missing artifact (EV-ADVIS-00531-01); neither party asserts a traffic cause and neither cites that identifier, so nothing turns on it and POL-RD-13 and POL-RD-10(5) are not engaged.\n\nThe fare — nothing to refund. This was an UPFRONT_QUOTE trip (EV-FARE-00531): quoted_fare_sgd 27.90 and total_charged 27.90, with charged_over_quote 0.00. Under POL-RD-11 the distance and time charges were fixed at booking, POL-RD-02 does not run, and the rider's only fare remedy is the excess of the charge over the quote plus properly chargeable pass-through lines — 27.90 less 27.90, which is 0.00, the ledger's own figure. There is no billing-integrity defect under POL-RD-09(4) either: the fare carries no ERP or toll lines, and the airport_surcharge line is 0.00. The platform charge of SGD 27.90, exactly the quoted price, is correct and stays. The driver's point that a Changi-origin surcharge is chargeable in principle is accepted, but it does not make the charge that was billed wrong.\n\nThe SGD 40 — proved as a demand, but outside the fare. The chat log (EV-CHAT-00531) shows the driver writing 'airport surcharge cannot in app, paynow 40 pls' and the rider replying 'ok sent'; the fare model does carry an airport surcharge for trips originating at Changi (SGD 6.00 off-peak, SGD 8.00 peak) with its own field on the ledger. The driver's premise that the app cannot handle the surcharge is therefore contradicted by the record, and describing the payment as simply voluntary does not survive that. But the SGD 40 was never a platform charge: it appears nowhere on the fare_breakdown. POL-RD-09(4) treats a surcharge as wrongly applied only where the trip does not ORIGINATE at an airport, and this trip originated at Changi Terminal 2, so no billing-integrity correction arises, and POL-RD-11(b) and POL-RD-09 cannot reach a payment made outside the app. I cannot order the SGD 40 refunded through the fare, and I do not convert an off-tariff private collection into a fare line. The open question — whether the driver should have solicited an off-app payment at all, on a representation the fare record contradicts — belongs here, in this explanation, as a matter for the platform's own review; it is not a referral, because no clause produces one.\n\nDriver-conduct referral under POL-RD-06(c) does not arise: deviation_flags_90d is 0 against 250 completed trips in 90 days, so no repeat-deviation pattern is established, and POL-RD-06 is not otherwise engaged. The rider's profile (deviation_claims_60d is 0) neither helps nor hurts her here, and under POL-GEN-01 every other field on either party's profile is context only. Escalation is not available: no POL-RD-10 trigger fires — the trace is unimpaired, the trip identifiers match across the trip record, trace and ledger, the timestamps are consistent, and no charge line is unmatched. The rider's fallback request to hold the disputed sum is declined for that reason.\n\nOutcome: NO_ACTION. The fare is correct and the present state stands."
